[[:db/retract 17592186228660 :answer-field/choices 17592186228661] [:db/retract 17592186228660 :answer-field/choices 17592186228662] [:db/retract 17592186228660 :answer-field/choices 17592186228663] [:db/retract 17592186228660 :answer-field/choices 17592186228664] [:db/retract 17592186228660 :answer-field/choices 17592186228665] {:db/id 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d1348d32-c72c-5639-a597-5810a3541a7f" "question-9876167b-d3d3-5f4c-9305-b455d21b66dc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229740 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y={u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-1)}\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s+1)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(-1+2)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-2+1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-1)f(t-1) & =u(t-1)[{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228660 :answer-field/correct "answer-acd86ce9-6dce-5fe4-9b28-94ed59469b8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea7ae7d0-cc80-5360-b492-20f511b7f34d" "answer-0e7b9c61-3e75-5f82-92cf-67afbe5b5737" "answer-7aa19d89-4802-59c5-aa41-c5e4815bad9b" "answer-acd86ce9-6dce-5fe4-9b28-94ed59469b8c" "answer-b3718138-b1f7-5833-85d1-e340c7cd8e54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea7ae7d0-cc80-5360-b492-20f511b7f34d" :answer/id #uuid "ea7ae7d0-cc80-5360-b492-20f511b7f34d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-(t-1)}+{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e7b9c61-3e75-5f82-92cf-67afbe5b5737" :answer/id #uuid "0e7b9c61-3e75-5f82-92cf-67afbe5b5737" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aa19d89-4802-59c5-aa41-c5e4815bad9b" :answer/id #uuid "7aa19d89-4802-59c5-aa41-c5e4815bad9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acd86ce9-6dce-5fe4-9b28-94ed59469b8c" :answer/id #uuid "acd86ce9-6dce-5fe4-9b28-94ed59469b8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3718138-b1f7-5833-85d1-e340c7cd8e54" :answer/id #uuid "b3718138-b1f7-5833-85d1-e340c7cd8e54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f427f52d-d8d0-506f-a966-8597af9a76fe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317274 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{2((t+2)-2)^{3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{2{t}^{3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317032 :answer-field/correct "answer-7ba17ec9-0c67-56cc-950e-450d8cd0068d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ba17ec9-0c67-56cc-950e-450d8cd0068d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ba17ec9-0c67-56cc-950e-450d8cd0068d" :answer/id #uuid "7ba17ec9-0c67-56cc-950e-450d8cd0068d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12e^{-2s}}{s^4(s+4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f427f52d-d8d0-506f-a966-8597af9a76fe" :question/id #uuid "f427f52d-d8d0-506f-a966-8597af9a76fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-342538" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\,,\\,y(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+2y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=5:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-5\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5{e}^{2t-6}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u(t-3)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{e}^{2(t+3)-6}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t){e}^{2t-6}\\} \\\\ (sY(s)-5)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)-5 & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =5+\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s+2}+\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{(s-2)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e48a5a69-87f8-5b78-872f-f1525fb7b704"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e48a5a69-87f8-5b78-872f-f1525fb7b704" :answer-field/id #uuid "e48a5a69-87f8-5b78-872f-f1525fb7b704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c6d68d2a-0474-5f05-8194-03e94a84b4a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6d68d2a-0474-5f05-8194-03e94a84b4a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6d68d2a-0474-5f05-8194-03e94a84b4a3" :answer/id #uuid "c6d68d2a-0474-5f05-8194-03e94a84b4a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{(s-2)(s+2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d1348d32-c72c-5639-a597-5810a3541a7f" :question/id #uuid "d1348d32-c72c-5639-a597-5810a3541a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,13A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=9A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=9\\,(-\\frac{2}{13})+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{9}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{13}(-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{2s+9}{{s}^{2}+9})=\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+3\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+2}\\}+2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}+3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d53ce85-93a3-5040-89c6-3357d95d5db8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d53ce85-93a3-5040-89c6-3357d95d5db8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d53ce85-93a3-5040-89c6-3357d95d5db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25a59e37-3156-5f96-a6d3-a085278a86ad" "answer-c5a09306-4232-5e19-a749-d592df6f358b" "answer-638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" "answer-072c003f-940f-5827-9c69-c6534a1061e9" "answer-ac923911-04d7-59bb-9992-7a8446c5e934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25a59e37-3156-5f96-a6d3-a085278a86ad" :answer/id #uuid "25a59e37-3156-5f96-a6d3-a085278a86ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5a09306-4232-5e19-a749-d592df6f358b" :answer/id #uuid "c5a09306-4232-5e19-a749-d592df6f358b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer/id #uuid "638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-072c003f-940f-5827-9c69-c6534a1061e9" :answer/id #uuid "072c003f-940f-5827-9c69-c6534a1061e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac923911-04d7-59bb-9992-7a8446c5e934" :answer/id #uuid "ac923911-04d7-59bb-9992-7a8446c5e934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9876167b-d3d3-5f4c-9305-b455d21b66dc" :question/id #uuid "9876167b-d3d3-5f4c-9305-b455d21b66dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342816" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(4t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s-3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n3=A({3}^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,25A=3\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=16A-3C\\,⟹\\,0=16\\,(\\frac{3}{25})-3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{25}(\\frac{3}{s-3}-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{25}(3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}+4\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3t}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ and $sin\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2235b94a-23b2-515a-9cb2-13dc47980c69"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2235b94a-23b2-515a-9cb2-13dc47980c69" :answer-field/id #uuid "2235b94a-23b2-515a-9cb2-13dc47980c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-61321b40-2ea0-5afa-9cb1-4cc94c8b9dae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c9f894b-0695-5fb5-a2fc-03e32b