wer-field/choices 17592186122691] [:db/retract 17592186122690 :answer-field/choices 17592186122692] [:db/retract 17592186122690 :answer-field/choices 17592186122693] [:db/retract 17592186122690 :answer-field/choices 17592186122694] [:db/retract 17592186122690 :answer-field/choices 17592186122695] [:db/retract 17592186121163 :answer-field/choices 17592186121168] [:db/retract 17592186121163 :answer-field/choices 17592186121164] [:db/retract 17592186121163 :answer-field/choices 17592186121165] [:db/retract 17592186121163 :answer-field/choices 17592186121166] [:db/retract 17592186121163 :answer-field/choices 17592186121167] [:db/retract 17592186124172 :answer-field/choices 17592186124176] [:db/retract 17592186124172 :answer-field/choices 17592186124177] [:db/retract 17592186124172 :answer-field/choices 17592186124173] [:db/retract 17592186124172 :answer-field/choices 17592186124174] [:db/retract 17592186124172 :answer-field/choices 17592186124175] {:db/id 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bf45d7d2-b32a-596a-b136-67ab888373db" "question-14bd3999-4f71-5f69-b9ed-e9c986ba9f80"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5c3d7252-78a8-50a4-b514-2643c3b47ad3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf45d7d2-b32a-596a-b136-67ab888373db" :question/id #uuid "bf45d7d2-b32a-596a-b136-67ab888373db" :question/math-academy-id "q-105767" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-2,4⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-5⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=4,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 4-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2,-5⟩-⟨-1,-2,4⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-9⟩ \\\\ 7 & =4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7.$ *" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2660f5ee-d91a-5b88-baed-9e30b77e6148"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2660f5ee-d91a-5b88-baed-9e30b77e6148" :answer-field/id #uuid "2660f5ee-d91a-5b88-baed-9e30b77e6148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbf20891-6146-5bf6-ba31-0c4e04a83100" "answer-392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" "answer-f5171abd-cddd-53a2-8365-f3f4701ed218" "answer-2681b92b-2fe3-55de-90b4-7056cbf32e80" "answer-82ece195-67e9-55ba-a35c-58754eb63f42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbf20891-6146-5bf6-ba31-0c4e04a83100" :answer/id #uuid "cbf20891-6146-5bf6-ba31-0c4e04a83100" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4{f}_{x}(c)+9{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" :answer/id #uuid "392f4eb9-7e9a-598f-bfef-2848170a47cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5171abd-cddd-53a2-8365-f3f4701ed218" :answer/id #uuid "f5171abd-cddd-53a2-8365-f3f4701ed218" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7{f}_{y}(c)-6{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2681b92b-2fe3-55de-90b4-7056cbf32e80" :answer/id #uuid "2681b92b-2fe3-55de-90b4-7056cbf32e80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ece195-67e9-55ba-a35c-58754eb63f42" :answer/id #uuid "82ece195-67e9-55ba-a35c-58754eb63f42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14bd3999-4f71-5f69-b9ed-e9c986ba9f80" :question/id #uuid "14bd3999-4f71-5f69-b9ed-e9c986ba9f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-105766" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,4⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=8,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 8-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,-1,3⟩-⟨1,0,4⟩) \\\\ 3 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 3 & ={f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3.$ *" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e4d565f-6191-5133-ae29-4a063b9a73e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e4d565f-6191-5133-ae29-4a063b9a73e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4d565f-6191-5133-ae29-4a063b9a73e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-852545d0-96ef-508b-83ae-f36cad069693" "answer-d1be7867-3292-5dc2-8849-8f2ba035929f" "answer-e7e12a8a-8a82-50af-b9be-2d1afca28f15" "answer-b3fb31b9-bbda-5e8c-9ef4-be73347fe4f1" "answer-52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-852545d0-96ef-508b-83ae-f36cad069693" :answer/id #uuid "852545d0-96ef-508b-83ae-f36cad069693" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=-13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1be7867-3292-5dc2-8849-8f2ba035929f" :answer/id #uuid "d1be7867-3292-5dc2-8849-8f2ba035929f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)+4{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7e12a8a-8a82-50af-b9be-2d1afca28f15" :answer/id #uuid "e7e12a8a-8a82-50af-b9be-2d1afca28f15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3fb31b9-bbda-5e8c-9ef4-be73347fe4f1" :answer/id #uuid "b3fb31b9-bbda-5e8c-9ef4-be73347fe4f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)-3{f}_{z}(c)=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9" :answer/id #uuid "52d2c808-6a32-5acf-8e51-89148e82ede9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125015 :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for \n $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})-({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},3{y}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =-3{c}_{1}^{2}+3{c}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x+1}{0+1} \\\\ y-1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({c}_{2}-2)^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 4{c}_{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}-2=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122690 :answer-field/correct "answer-009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" :answer-field/choices ["answer-009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" "answer-3698d5a3-a25a-593a-975b-351b9af63bfb" "answer-78dc3ae4-aaae-53b9-a44a-1ae2307a7917" "answer-8cb94ca4-f440-5612-bb29-2301fce49975" "answer-9efd85af-9e07-5549-b93f-6870a2b05040"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" :answer/id #uuid "009b6ee6-cb03-533c-b05a-a8d696a75880" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3698d5a3-a25a-593a-975b-351b9af63bfb" :answer/id #uuid "3698d5a3-a25a-593a-975b-351b9af63bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78dc3ae4-aaae-53b9-a44a-1ae2307a7917" :answer/id #uuid "78dc3ae4-aaae-53b9-a44a-1ae2307a7917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{8}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cb94ca4-f440-5612-bb29-2301fce49975" :answer/id #uuid "8cb94ca4-f440-5612-bb29-2301fce49975" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{7}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9efd85af-9e07-5549-b93f-6870a2b05040" :answer/id #uuid "9efd85af-9e07-5549-b93f-6870a2b05040" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{7}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c3d7252-78a8-50a4-b514-2643c3b47ad3" :question/id #uuid "5c3d7252-78a8-50a4-b514-2643c3b47ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106075" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({1}^{2}+3)-({0}^{2}+1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =2{c}_{1}+2 \\\\ 1 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{2},2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59557dca-7f7b-53f1-95d6-4b59fe6e4251"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59557dca-7f7b-53f1-95d6-4b59fe6e4251" :answer-field/id #uuid "59557dca-7f7b-53f1-95d6-4b59fe6e4251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" :answer-field/choices ["answer-724aaf30-aac6-5e20-acb1-5fa47f134fe4" "answer-8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" "answer-ebb4def9-72b8-5730-ab1a-af5ca0f9d64a" "answer-162b92da-df23-5c30-ad49-2fd32f33a03e" "answer-295bfcf1-2a63-541e-8767-7a6693f4baa2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-724aaf30-aac6-5e20-acb1-5fa47f134fe4" :answer/id #uuid "724aaf30-aac6-5e20-acb1-5fa47f134fe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" :answer/id #uuid "8053c7ab-909b-5f33-9ce9-0d798cb23006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb4def9-72b8-5730-ab1a-af5ca0f9d64a" :answer/id #uuid "ebb4def9-72b8-5730-ab1a-af5ca0f9d64a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-162b92da-df23-5c30-ad49-2fd32f33a03e" :answer/id #uuid "162b92da-df23-5c30-ad49-2fd32f33a03e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-295bfcf1-2a63-541e-8767-7a6693f4baa2" :answer/id #uuid "295bfcf1-2a63-541e-8767-7a6693f4baa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124761 :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2},-z,-y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{1} \\\\ x & =y=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t,\\,z=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-t-t & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(3t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121163 :answer-field/correct "answer-50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1cfcf18-3023-5d0a-9e55-6fde879778df" "answer-50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" "answer-c298df37-cd11-54c8-a6ae-8244b42ec27a" "answer-c92734c5-3437-5572-a60d-19e95c9b9d36" "answer-07f8a323-c0be-535b-948e-c78114672e3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1cfcf18-3023-5d0a-9e55-6fde879778df" :answer/id #uuid "e1cfcf18-3023-5d0a-9e55-6fde879778df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" :answer/id #uuid "50cf239c-b6f5-566d-958b-b55860061ad8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c298df37-cd11-54c8-a6ae-8244b42ec27a" :answer/id #uuid "c298df37-cd11-54c8-a6ae-8244b42ec27a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c92734c5-3437-5572-a60d-19e95c9b9d36" :answer/id #uuid "c92734c5-3437-5572-a60d-19e95c9b9d36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07f8a323-c0be-535b-948e-c78114672e3f" :answer/id #uuid "07f8a323-c0be-535b-948e-c78114672e3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125262 :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3,2)-f(0,1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨0,1,1⟩) \\\\ 3-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4z,-2y,4x+2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{3}-4{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2t+1,\\,z=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=2t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3=4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-4(2t+1)+6(t+1) & =3 \\\\ 4t-8t-4+6t+6 & =3 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+1=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124172 :answer-field/correct "answer-fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d6bd118-2348-5c1b-9b4e-580c8ff5121b" "answer-e256dd70-be6c-5fd9-94cd-963d6af2d61d" "answer-1d15fa8a-7adb-517a-a824-c1cc2486e352" "answer-fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" "answer-55f88006-7ccf-567b-a57c-fe1c317bd9da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d6bd118-2348-5c1b-9b4e-580c8ff5121b" :answer/id #uuid "1d6bd118-2348-5c1b-9b4e-580c8ff5121b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e256dd70-be6c-5fd9-94cd-963d6af2d61d" :answer/id #uuid "e256dd70-be6c-5fd9-94cd-963d6af2d61d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d15fa8a-7adb-517a-a824-c1cc2486e352" :answer/id #uuid "1d15fa8a-7adb-517a-a824-c1cc2486e352" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" :answer/id #uuid "fe14b2e0-69de-5fbe-9a68-76ef240bc41a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f88006-7ccf-567b-a57c-fe1c317bd9da" :answer/id #uuid "55f88006-7ccf-567b-a57c-fe1c317bd9da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124553 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f8639b5-fe7e-594e-a639-5523842bd2ae" "question-240c650f-50a8-531d-9d84-394b65ab5cda" "question-54914efc-dfc4-51a5-a306-07132cb10e69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cdadd58a-68d8-50fc-902f-9f8e63435f08"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f8639b5-fe7e-594e-a639-5523842bd2ae" :question/id #uuid "4f8639b5-fe7e-594e-a639-5523842bd2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71206" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $H$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},5) & =\\frac{6}{5-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 2 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-851cb76b-3dab-5e83-b29a-5e7d934c5feb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-851cb76b-3dab-5e83-b29a-5e7d934c5feb" :answer-field/id #uuid "851cb76b-3dab-5e83-b29a-5e7d934c5feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4d78e38-b8a2-5c8a-b4e9-0d71b7c101a4" "answer-e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" "answer-c1451722-3327-5387-bdcd-bea771d52fbf" "answer-7d6bf85a-0106-582b-b833-b0e53d86d82d" "answer-f937e620-b64a-58df-bc8a-1ab2197c79b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4d78e38-b8a2-5c8a-b4e9-0d71b7c101a4" :answer/id #uuid "f4d78e38-b8a2-5c8a-b4e9-0d71b7c101a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" :answer/id #uuid "e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1451722-3327-5387-bdcd-bea771d52fbf" :answer/id #uuid "c1451722-3327-5387-bdcd-bea771d52fbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d6bf85a-0106-582b-b833-b0e53d86d82d" :answer/id #uuid "7d6bf85a-0106-582b-b833-b0e53d86d82d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f937e620-b64a-58df-bc8a-1ab2197c79b5" :answer/id #uuid "f937e620-b64a-58df-bc8a-1ab2197c79b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-240c650f-50a8-531d-9d84-394b65ab5cda" :question/id #uuid "240c650f-50a8-531d-9d84-394b65ab5cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-128449" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+2}{1-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =\\frac{3(0)+2}{1-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{1-3y} & =-1 \\\\ 3x+2 & =-(1-3y) \\\\ 3x+2 & =3y-1 \\\\ 3y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the line $y=x+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da5b101c-53ff-558b-b46b-69d15ea46f42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da5b101c-53ff-558b-b46b-69d15ea46f42" :answer-field/id #uuid "da5b101c-53ff-558b-b46b-69d15ea46f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9726429c-deeb-55e4-8059-876872148727" "answer-50711eab-a9fd-54ec-87bd-c694182fb1bf" "answer-31f8d85c-0956-5e4d-bcef-82effb0ac504" "answer-4588c3b3-aaa7-5ee7-838b-26b6a186aee7" "answer-c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9726429c-deeb-55e4-8059-876872148727" :answer/id #uuid "9726429c-deeb-55e4-8059-876872148727" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50711eab-a9fd-54ec-87bd-c694182fb1bf" :answer/id #uuid "50711eab-a9fd-54ec-87bd-c694182fb1bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31f8d85c-0956-5e4d-bcef-82effb0ac504" :answer/id #uuid "31f8d85c-0956-5e4d-bcef-82effb0ac504" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4588c3b3-aaa7-5ee7-838b-26b6a186aee7" :answer/id #uuid "4588c3b3-aaa7-5ee7-838b-26b6a186aee7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d" :answer/id #uuid "c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54914efc-dfc4-51a5-a306-07132cb10e69" :question/id #uuid "54914efc-dfc4-51a5-a306-07132cb10e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-71219" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $F$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{7},1) & =\\frac{12(1)}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-{1}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 6y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+9 & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-617bb124-5500-5301-bf14-9faf85d05ab0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-617bb124-5500-5301-bf14-9faf85d05ab0" :answer-field/id #uuid "617bb124-5500-5301-bf14-9faf85d05ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d1e08ab-ab16-5e9e-ae19-5b3fa9994835" "answer-c1680052-ff70-539b-89f5-342b49b41b70" "answer-0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" "answer-714afbc6-420e-5bbb-9931-29075927e5dd" "answer-6c7e143d-8279-5647-a451-67118e0765ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d1e08ab-ab16-5e9e-ae19-5b3fa9994835" :answer/id #uuid "0d1e08ab-ab16-5e9e-ae19-5b3fa9994835" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}-{y}^{2}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1680052-ff70-539b-89f5-342b49b41b70" :answer/id #uuid "c1680052-ff70-539b-89f5-342b49b41b70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x-3={y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" :answer/id #uuid "0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-714afbc6-420e-5bbb-9931-29075927e5dd" :answer/id #uuid "714afbc6-420e-5bbb-9931-29075927e5dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c7e143d-8279-5647-a451-67118e0765ff" :answer/id #uuid "6c7e143d-8279-5647-a451-67118e0765ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdadd58a-68d8-50fc-902f-9f8e63435f08" :question/id #uuid "cdadd58a-68d8-50fc-902f-9f8e63435f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-129057" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =c({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(1-c){y}^{2} & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse. So, I is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curves are $x=\\pm 1,$ which consists of two lines. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3,$ which is a hyperbola. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30824be3-9d05-5e38-9bc5-879edf4e9627"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30824be3-9d05-5e38-9bc5-879edf4e9627" :answer-field/id #uuid "30824be3-9d05-5e38-9bc5-879edf4e9627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6adb7ddd-b8f8-5210-b2dc-a69eb6349e13" "answer-04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" "answer-90fc163a-b462-532a-8c90-4c1d22cba43a" "answer-31f95b5b-7ede-5e9d-8f9b-f6d9bb837662" "answer-9c84aac5-2157-5af5-9026-ef9eb0365bc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6adb7ddd-b8f8-5210-b2dc-a69eb6349e13" :answer/id #uuid "6adb7ddd-b8f8-5210-b2dc-a69eb6349e13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" :answer/id #uuid "04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90fc163a-b462-532a-8c90-4c1d22cba43a" :answer/id #uuid "90fc163a-b462-532a-8c90-4c1d22cba43a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31f95b5b-7ede-5e9d-8f9b-f6d9bb837662" :answer/id #uuid "31f95b5b-7ede-5e9d-8f9b-f6d9bb837662" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c84aac5-2157-5af5-9026-ef9eb0365bc8" :answer/id #uuid "9c84aac5-2157-5af5-9026-ef9eb0365bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186123560 :answer-field/choices 17592186123562] [:db/retract 17592186123560 :answer-field/choices 17592186123563] [:db/retract 17592186123560 :answer-field/choices 17592186123565] {:db/id 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fc61e8f1-0b37-52d2-8620-d0e767062e04" "question-fdb51987-aeea-5d6d-9fcd-a012fef3d69f" "question-0883765a-f056-556c-bee6-eafc65b5e91c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124386 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\dfrac 1 4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8006767d-6578-52a2-aa78-8300e8ed36b3" "question-0f04e402-41a0-5006-8eb9-65c2d82f6e13"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124387 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3e8207e-9140-5f5f-bfa7-ab83fc0d1420" "question-1b8c4518-97e5-5ab3-a859-e015cbefbbce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124286 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a8882e4-c45a-5dc1-b53b-97925de4eb41" "question-940dd7e5-a02e-5ae8-8d31-37203bbfab5d" "question-0443135d-0cff-565f-8ee8-219a9e567bef" "question-2f421385-b333-57a6-99f9-5c22d85a1c43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124289 :question/problem "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc61e8f1-0b37-52d2-8620-d0e767062e04" :question/id #uuid "fc61e8f1-0b37-52d2-8620-d0e767062e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-334180" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7f1d5de28c6479a2e9b8b33a6da25a4091b71b58f6dc0c535ef390c4cb97799.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71c427a6-d5aa-56d6-bd23-df19a31d1d11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71c427a6-d5aa-56d6-bd23-df19a31d1d11" :answer-field/id #uuid "71c427a6-d5aa-56d6-bd23-df19a31d1d11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/id #uuid "4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fdb51987-aeea-5d6d-9fcd-a012fef3d69f" :question/id #uuid "fdb51987-aeea-5d6d-9fcd-a012fef3d69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78081" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a3143b2c-7af2-5445-aaba-827a5cd2b88e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a3143b2c-7af2-5445-aaba-827a5cd2b88e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3143b2c-7af2-5445-aaba-827a5cd2b88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a65e4a14-81ca-54eb-9501-b356ec107bdf" "answer-55a68899-fd6b-5e51-8741-e8646384dc54" "answer-6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" "answer-d7f23a02-6b5e-5b17-bde8-5879ecbee74d" "answer-1758365f-a72d-56cb-8910-850f490ea058"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a65e4a14-81ca-54eb-9501-b356ec107bdf" :answer/id #uuid "a65e4a14-81ca-54eb-9501-b356ec107bdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a68899-fd6b-5e51-8741-e8646384dc54" :answer/id #uuid "55a68899-fd6b-5e51-8741-e8646384dc54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/id #uuid "6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7f23a02-6b5e-5b17-bde8-5879ecbee74d" :answer/id #uuid "d7f23a02-6b5e-5b17-bde8-5879ecbee74d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1758365f-a72d-56cb-8910-850f490ea058" :answer/id #uuid "1758365f-a72d-56cb-8910-850f490ea058" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0883765a-f056-556c-bee6-eafc65b5e91c" :question/id #uuid "0883765a-f056-556c-bee6-eafc65b5e91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334164" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f3879b6-ecd9-5bd8-a6a5-eca6fd88dfd9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f3879b6-ecd9-5bd8-a6a5-eca6fd88dfd9" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3879b6-ecd9-5bd8-a6a5-eca6fd88dfd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/id #uuid "b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8006767d-6578-52a2-aa78-8300e8ed36b3" :question/id #uuid "8006767d-6578-52a2-aa78-8300e8ed36b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334193" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb2cc812-d3f1-5ddb-9bec-85884d6fb255"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb2cc812-d3f1-5ddb-9bec-85884d6fb255" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2cc812-d3f1-5ddb-9bec-85884d6fb255" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer-field/choices ["answer-612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/id #uuid "612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f04e402-41a0-5006-8eb9-65c2d82f6e13" :question/id #uuid "0f04e402-41a0-5006-8eb9-65c2d82f6e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-300983" :question/problem "![](images/64/6469991ee6751110f55ce6462c4c3f2e406d8b61fabcb6c2d2cc1515ec12dd2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=$\n\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3f15e0b-05f8-5013-b9b0-8f30387b49a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3f15e0b-05f8-5013-b9b0-8f30387b49a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f15e0b-05f8-5013-b9b0-8f30387b49a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/id #uuid "fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3e8207e-9140-5f5f-bfa7-ab83fc0d1420" :question/id #uuid "e3e8207e-9140-5f5f-bfa7-ab83fc0d1420" :question/math-academy-id "q-334233" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db32905c-5c7a-5bd1-bd14-25355dd7bb61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db32905c-5c7a-5bd1-bd14-25355dd7bb61" :answer-field/id #uuid "db32905c-5c7a-5bd1-bd14-25355dd7bb61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/id #uuid "7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b8c4518-97e5-5ab3-a859-e015cbefbbce" :question/id #uuid "1b8c4518-97e5-5ab3-a859-e015cbefbbce" :question/math-academy-id "q-36453" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f63655d-d2a8-5b20-b3ce-1835b71b6875"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f63655d-d2a8-5b20-b3ce-1835b71b6875" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f63655d-d2a8-5b20-b3ce-1835b71b6875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9849d06c-d6fa-5f44-b558-a71dc686c4f4" "answer-c447fc46-df17-5027-b37f-7ea6e75b40a9" "answer-70c09658-cf29-5193-8d12-ec120b53f9f1" "answer-43429be9-bb0e-5b18-86bb-fbdf79bb94bf" "answer-46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9849d06c-d6fa-5f44-b558-a71dc686c4f4" :answer/id #uuid "9849d06c-d6fa-5f44-b558-a71dc686c4f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c447fc46-df17-5027-b37f-7ea6e75b40a9" :answer/id #uuid "c447fc46-df17-5027-b37f-7ea6e75b40a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70c09658-cf29-5193-8d12-ec120b53f9f1" :answer/id #uuid "70c09658-cf29-5193-8d12-ec120b53f9f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43429be9-bb0e-5b18-86bb-fbdf79bb94bf" :answer/id #uuid "43429be9-bb0e-5b18-86bb-fbdf79bb94bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/id #uuid "46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a8882e4-c45a-5dc1-b53b-97925de4eb41" :question/id #uuid "7a8882e4-c45a-5dc1-b53b-97925de4eb41" :question/math-academy-id "q-46970" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\,\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\frac{3}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa92c457186fbdf71552ec79c782af7983e6fa09009e7f0efdd02b8f74e3ab98.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({0}^{+})}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty \n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d71e7b0d-88ab-52d1-8e79-593766af594d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d71e7b0d-88ab-52d1-8e79-593766af594d" :answer-field/id #uuid "d71e7b0d-88ab-52d1-8e79-593766af594d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b0366d3-18b5-5880-ab15-32e4c49fb81c" "answer-2b422786-3e36-56eb-94b9-64e09e6899fb" "answer-459108ee-9fb7-5d34-8d66-9abacde67ea5" "answer-3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" "answer-1e9048a8-fb8e-50a8-9d64-a493cee42eea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b0366d3-18b5-5880-ab15-32e4c49fb81c" :answer/id #uuid "4b0366d3-18b5-5880-ab15-32e4c49fb81c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b422786-3e36-56eb-94b9-64e09e6899fb" :answer/id #uuid "2b422786-3e36-56eb-94b9-64e09e6899fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-459108ee-9fb7-5d34-8d66-9abacde67ea5" :answer/id #uuid "459108ee-9fb7-5d34-8d66-9abacde67ea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" :answer/id #uuid "3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e9048a8-fb8e-50a8-9d64-a493cee42eea" :answer/id #uuid "1e9048a8-fb8e-50a8-9d64-a493cee42eea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-940dd7e5-a02e-5ae8-8d31-37203bbfab5d" :question/id #uuid "940dd7e5-a02e-5ae8-8d31-37203bbfab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-305776" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}})\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8c07c0a-9297-5a81-902b-be41db3eb020"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8c07c0a-9297-5a81-902b-be41db3eb020" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c07c0a-9297-5a81-902b-be41db3eb020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/id #uuid "3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0443135d-0cff-565f-8ee8-219a9e567bef" :question/id #uuid "0443135d-0cff-565f-8ee8-219a9e567bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-99351" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}})=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}:$\n\n![](images/ae/aeeed013b57f5fd8a554ca6613bf9e3fa9419ae2e0fd5920397c5b9cb0dd88fb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{5}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b673d111-8088-5a19-9579-504b507d8a88"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b673d111-8088-5a19-9579-504b507d8a88" :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d111-8088-5a19-9579-504b507d8a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-360b1df4-8a51-5c90-827b-ce21bb471dc6" "answer-5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" "answer-0f7ee46e-dc63-58c3-89bb-875458d30291" "answer-500e4e0d-01de-5798-a08b-46e3ec45ecf9" "answer-63379f06-acca-58df-bcc7-05308d492f18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-360b1df4-8a51-5c90-827b-ce21bb471dc6" :answer/id #uuid "360b1df4-8a51-5c90-827b-ce21bb471dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/id #uuid "5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7ee46e-dc63-58c3-89bb-875458d30291" :answer/id #uuid "0f7ee46e-dc63-58c3-89bb-875458d30291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-500e4e0d-01de-5798-a08b-46e3ec45ecf9" :answer/id #uuid "500e4e0d-01de-5798-a08b-46e3ec45ecf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63379f06-acca-58df-bcc7-05308d492f18" :answer/id #uuid "63379f06-acca-58df-bcc7-05308d492f18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f421385-b333-57a6-99f9-5c22d85a1c43" :question/id #uuid "2f421385-b333-57a6-99f9-5c22d85a1c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-334297" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffedd1b83fa2d80b924b0d2f68a3100236347e3222ec171bc60b299579a7af4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-142b5723-947e-572c-b68d-13e6c9995afd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-142b5723-947e-572c-b68d-13e6c9995afd" :answer-field/id #uuid "142b5723-947e-572c-b68d-13e6c9995afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b" :answer/id #uuid "404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125160 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123560 :answer-field/correct "answer-e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d34d34c-9884-5f4e-af4d-aaa1afcf0af4" "answer-e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" "answer-fa250561-2f81-526b-9bb1-37d1f2aa9f88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d34d34c-9884-5f4e-af4d-aaa1afcf0af4" :answer/id #uuid "3d34d34c-9884-5f4e-af4d-aaa1afcf0af4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" :answer/id #uuid "e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa250561-2f81-526b-9bb1-37d1f2aa9f88" :answer/id #uuid "fa250561-2f81-526b-9bb1-37d1f2aa9f88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125756 :tutorial/content "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is because as $x$ increases, any polynomial $P(x)$ will grow without bound, and therefore its reciprocal shrinks to zero.\n\nFor example, the expression $x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^3 - x^2 + 1}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the expression $(2x-5)^4$ can be multiplied out to become a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{ (2x-5)^4 }\\right)  = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125720 :tutorial/content "Let's again look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n},$ where $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ and consequently the limit behavior is the same.  For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=\\infty , \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125721 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ (where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124705 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129153 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcd7f2f3-3373-5879-b2c8-71cda166f322" "question-d38f76f8-d0d4-57e2-9294-46487229e4bf" "question-5e519634-3084-59a8-9ded-27e7f0393295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcd7f2f3-3373-5879-b2c8-71cda166f322" :question/id #uuid "dcd7f2f3-3373-5879-b2c8-71cda166f322" :question/math-academy-id "q-39641" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cf50074bd5b43815dd03e88db20086dcdefb42f4e01d30e37be014fa80d772f.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-63374499-66b5-5791-922b-661d7b0c8caa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-63374499-66b5-5791-922b-661d7b0c8caa" :answer-field/id #uuid "63374499-66b5-5791-922b-661d7b0c8caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bcf70b2d-7723-5385-9a7b-d819feef6e27" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7f32bf6-9280-584c-a520-3a0e88b30fb7" "answer-bcf70b2d-7723-5385-9a7b-d819feef6e27" "answer-7fa3415f-dbfc-5f8e-9af1-761abd655bf7" "answer-dc910607-aca8-5f9d-8d6a-45d5681459bd" "answer-1f66b003-3d9a-5775-8f34-39e274aa4aae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7f32bf6-9280-584c-a520-3a0e88b30fb7" :answer/id #uuid "e7f32bf6-9280-584c-a520-3a0e88b30fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcf70b2d-7723-5385-9a7b-d819feef6e27" :answer/id #uuid "bcf70b2d-7723-5385-9a7b-d819feef6e27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fa3415f-dbfc-5f8e-9af1-761abd655bf7" :answer/id #uuid "7fa3415f-dbfc-5f8e-9af1-761abd655bf7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc910607-aca8-5f9d-8d6a-45d5681459bd" :answer/id #uuid "dc910607-aca8-5f9d-8d6a-45d5681459bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f66b003-3d9a-5775-8f34-39e274aa4aae" :answer/id #uuid "1f66b003-3d9a-5775-8f34-39e274aa4aae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d38f76f8-d0d4-57e2-9294-46487229e4bf" :question/id #uuid "d38f76f8-d0d4-57e2-9294-46487229e4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-89862" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5b42df6ce8884f482ef982d5bb74ff6fd10fe0b69c6c3f6d8cbd2bc1f11336a.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7ec1097-ac95-5fe7-9c75-36d7809606ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7ec1097-ac95-5fe7-9c75-36d7809606ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ec1097-ac95-5fe7-9c75-36d7809606ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ede2bd5f-efe8-570f-8060-9c49f87d7ac0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffd580bb-7fbf-5757-a6fd-13d33f363c4d" "answer-0349f7aa-beab-57d7-809c-a555c0b95876" "answer-ede2bd5f-efe8-570f-8060-9c49f87d7ac0" "answer-67ee4313-1903-5371-95b1-73a63b431377" "answer-7aaac658-c6aa-5227-bd30-a1731ca7adf3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffd580bb-7fbf-5757-a6fd-13d33f363c4d" :answer/id #uuid "ffd580bb-7fbf-5757-a6fd-13d33f363c4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0349f7aa-beab-57d7-809c-a555c0b95876" :answer/id #uuid "0349f7aa-beab-57d7-809c-a555c0b95876" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ede2bd5f-efe8-570f-8060-9c49f87d7ac0" :answer/id #uuid "ede2bd5f-efe8-570f-8060-9c49f87d7ac0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ee4313-1903-5371-95b1-73a63b431377" :answer/id #uuid "67ee4313-1903-5371-95b1-73a63b431377" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aaac658-c6aa-5227-bd30-a1731ca7adf3" :answer/id #uuid "7aaac658-c6aa-5227-bd30-a1731ca7adf3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e519634-3084-59a8-9ded-27e7f0393295" :question/id #uuid "5e519634-3084-59a8-9ded-27e7f0393295" :question/math-academy-id "q-103610" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f7a49cdcbca9c5488c7e607f68aa161de67d9b1ff7c654afe6dafaafc3f18f4.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nNone of the given options have all of the above properties. Therefore, none of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5f6a4ffc-69e4-5d36-ac0e-43d4e95b6db4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f6a4ffc-69e4-5d36-ac0e-43d4e95b6db4" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f6a4ffc-69e4-5d36-ac0e-43d4e95b6db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a49cc061-2715-53ce-a76f-c933d350a603" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ce5d138-0fea-5b84-a4c3-7e0d18904bad" "answer-f8c00b07-8e97-5887-a0e1-871fd0e4bf08" "answer-ecc6d8fe-36be-595f-a3e7-8dde7d7a4e66" "answer-0cf7eae6-d38e-52a4-b908-c62b591dd2ca" "answer-a49cc061-2715-53ce-a76f-c933d350a603"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ce5d138-0fea-5b84-a4c3-7e0d18904bad" :answer/id #uuid "5ce5d138-0fea-5b84-a4c3-7e0d18904bad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8c00b07-8e97-5887-a0e1-871fd0e4bf08" :answer/id #uuid "f8c00b07-8e97-5887-a0e1-871fd0e4bf08" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecc6d8fe-36be-595f-a3e7-8dde7d7a4e66" :answer/id #uuid "ecc6d8fe-36be-595f-a3e7-8dde7d7a4e66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cf7eae6-d38e-52a4-b908-c62b591dd2ca" :answer/id #uuid "0cf7eae6-d38e-52a4-b908-c62b591dd2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a49cc061-2715-53ce-a76f-c933d350a603" :answer/id #uuid "a49cc061-2715-53ce-a76f-c933d350a603" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-60f54a6c-78b9-5814-9af3-2338be509387" :activity/id #uuid "60f54a6c-78b9-5814-9af3-2338be509387" :activity/title "Graphing the Square Root Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050753 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-ec804f95-79f8-56a7-b957-bed19bc7d31d" "step-947c67f9-8e75-5571-bd46-01f2a3574e64" "step-b80f8ef3-405e-579c-a6a3-175c88843334"] :activity/first-step "step-ec804f95-79f8-56a7-b957-bed19bc7d31d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ec804f95-79f8-56a7-b957-bed19bc7d31d" :step/id #uuid "ec804f95-79f8-56a7-b957-bed19bc7d31d" :step/content "question-dcd7f2f3-3373-5879-b2c8-71cda166f322" :step/next "step-947c67f9-8e75-5571-bd46-01f2a3574e64" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-947c67f9-8e75-5571-bd46-01f2a3574e64" :step/id #uuid "947c67f9-8e75-5571-bd46-01f2a3574e64" :step/content "question-d38f76f8-d0d4-57e2-9294-46487229e4bf" :step/next "step-b80f8ef3-405e-579c-a6a3-175c88843334" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b80f8ef3-405e-579c-a6a3-175c88843334" :step/id #uuid "b80f8ef3-405e-579c-a6a3-175c88843334" :step/content "question-5e519634-3084-59a8-9ded-27e7f0393295" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186134101 :question/answer-fields 17592186130762] {:db/id 17592186369525 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (2-\\frac{1}{2})-(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134101 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
